{"seeAlsoSections":[{"title":"Explanation","identifiers":["doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/PluginArchitecture","doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/DesignPrinciples","doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/SecurityPosture"],"anchor":"Explanation","generated":true}],"sections":[],"variants":[{"paths":["\/documentation\/swidux\/architectureguide"],"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}]}],"primaryContentSections":[{"content":[{"text":"Overview","type":"heading","anchor":"Overview","level":2},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Swidux relies on specific patterns to ensure correct ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"@Observable"},{"text":" behavior, efficient rendering, and safe concurrency. This article explains the reasoning behind these patterns.","type":"text"}]},{"text":"The Snapshot Pattern","type":"heading","anchor":"The-Snapshot-Pattern","level":2},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Store.send()"},{"text":" copies the observer tree into a local struct, mutates the copy via the reducer, then assigns changed properties back. This happens internally via ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"SwiduxObservable"},{"text":" — ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"State(observer:)"},{"text":" packs, ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"State.apply(_:to:)"},{"text":" unpacks.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"The pattern exists for two reasons:","type":"text"}]},{"items":[{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"inlineContent":[{"code":"@Observable","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" equality checking.","type":"text"}],"type":"strong"},{"text":" The ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"set"},{"text":" accessor checks ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Equatable"},{"text":" and suppresses no-op notifications. Swift’s ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"_modify"},{"text":" accessor (used by ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"inout"},{"text":") fires notifications unconditionally. The snapshot pattern routes through ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"set"},{"text":".","type":"text"}]}]},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Cross-slice observation isolation.","type":"text"}],"type":"strong"},{"text":" The ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"@Swidux"},{"text":" macro generates a separate ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"@Observable"},{"text":" class for each ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"@Slice"},{"text":" property. A view reading ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"store.items"},{"text":" won’t re-render when only ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"store.ui"},{"text":" changes, because they live on different observer objects.","type":"text"}]}]}],"type":"orderedList"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"The ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"@Swidux"},{"text":" macro generates the observer class tree and ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"SwiduxObservable"},{"text":" conformance automatically. Hand-written conformance is still possible for advanced cases — see ","type":"text"},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/SwiduxObservable","isActive":true},{"text":".","type":"text"}]},{"style":"note","type":"aside","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Explicit equality guards (","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"if x != state.x { x = state.x }"},{"text":") are unnecessary. ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"@Observable"},{"text":" already checks equality on ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"set"},{"text":". Unconditional assignment is safe.","type":"text"}]}],"name":"Note"},{"text":"Dispatch Loop Detection","type":"heading","anchor":"Dispatch-Loop-Detection","level":2},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/PersistencePlugin","isActive":true},{"text":" warns if more than 100 changes are drained within a single debounce interval (the ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"loopThreshold"},{"text":" parameter; 250 ms by default). It counts only dispatches that changed an ","type":"text"},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/EntityStore","isActive":true},{"text":", and it reports once per burst — the count starts over after a whole debounce interval with no drains. This usually means an effect or plugin dispatches an action on every state change, feeding the cycle it reacts to. A steady stream of edits, such as a slider drag, stays well under the threshold and is not reported.","type":"text"}]},{"text":"Debounced Persistence","type":"heading","anchor":"Debounced-Persistence","level":2},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Each drain restarts the flush’s debounce timer, so a burst of edits is written once. The timer is never pushed further than ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"maxWait"},{"text":" past the oldest pending change (four debounce intervals, and at least a second, by default), so continuous edits still reach storage while they continue. A retry of a failed flush keeps its own schedule; a new edit postpones it only when the flush it schedules is due first and carries the failed batch anyway.","type":"text"}]},{"text":"Reducer Weight","type":"heading","anchor":"Reducer-Weight","level":2},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Reducers run synchronously on the MainActor. Move O(n²) work into an ","type":"text"},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/Effect","isActive":true},{"text":" and dispatch the result back as an action.","type":"text"}]},{"text":"Effect Threading","type":"heading","anchor":"Effect-Threading","level":2},{"style":"important","type":"aside","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Effects run on Swift concurrency’s cooperative thread pool via ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Task { @concurrent in }"},{"text":". Blocking calls (","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Process.waitUntilExit()"},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"DispatchSemaphore.wait()"},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Thread.sleep()"},{"text":") hold threads hostage. If enough block, the pool starves and the MainActor freezes.","type":"text"}]}],"name":"Important"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Use async alternatives: ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"terminationHandler"},{"text":" + continuation instead of ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"waitUntilExit()"},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Task.sleep()"},{"text":" instead of ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Thread.sleep()"},{"text":", async file I\/O instead of ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Data(contentsOf:)"},{"text":".","type":"text"}]}],"kind":"content"}],"identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/ArchitectureGuide","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"schemaVersion":{"patch":0,"major":0,"minor":3},"metadata":{"modules":[{"name":"Swidux"}],"roleHeading":"Article","role":"article","title":"Architecture & Performance"},"kind":"article","hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux"]]},"abstract":[{"text":"Understand the architectural patterns that make Swidux work correctly with SwiftUI’s observation system.","type":"text"}],"references":{"doc://Swidux/documentation/Swidux/DesignPrinciples":{"title":"Design Principles","identifier":"doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/DesignPrinciples","kind":"article","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The 10 rules that shape every Swidux app, and the reasoning behind them."}],"type":"topic","role":"article","url":"\/documentation\/swidux\/designprinciples"},"doc://Swidux/documentation/Swidux/EntityStore":{"abstract":[{"text":"An ordered, keyed collection of iden